<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scipio Aemilianus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 185–129 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scipio Aemilianus was a Roman general and statesman who commanded the final siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. In 146 BCE he captured and destroyed the city. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> treat this as the end of Carthage as a state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus.jpg" alt="Scipio Aemilianus" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_statue_of_a_Hellenistic_prince,_1st_half_of_2nd_century_BC,_found_on_the_Quirinal_in_Rome,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(31479801364).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Scipio Aemilianus was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus Macedonicus</a>, the victor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a>, and his first wife, Papiria Masonis. He was adopted by his first cousin Publius Cornelius Scipio, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus</a>, who had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. At seventeen he took part in his father's campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Plutarch relates that when he was missing after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a> the whole army searched for him, and that he returned late, covered with the blood of the enemies he had slain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 151 BCE, when Roman recruitment for the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> had collapsed, he asked the Senate to send him there as a military tribune or legate, and his example led others to volunteer. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> he was a military tribune who repeatedly saved Roman forces from disaster. In 147 BCE he was elected consul although under the legal age and was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> without the usual drawing of lots. After a year of fighting he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and made about 50,000 survivors prisoner, roughly one tenth of the city's population. On the Senate's orders he had the city burnt and razed. It was formerly believed that he also salted the ground, but modern scholars have found no evidence of that.</p><p>Elected consul again in 134 BCE, he took command in the Numantine War, restored discipline in a demoralised army and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> with a circuit of fortifications nine kilometres long. The Numantines were starved into surrender, and Scipio sold the survivors into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and destroyed the city. He served as censor in 142 BCE. A patron of writers and philosophers, he gathered the Scipionic circle, which included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Panaetius, Lucilius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terence">Terence</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Scipio disliked the land reforms of his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus">Tiberius Gracchus</a>, and he became unpopular when he took the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Italian allies against the commission applying the land law of Gracchus. He died at home in 129 BCE, and rumours of murder circulated, naming Cornelia, Sempronia, Fulvius Flaccus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus">Gaius Gracchus</a> and Gaius Papirius Carbo. Modern historians see no strong evidence for any cause but natural death. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Scipio wept at the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and quoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s lines on the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, saying he feared the same fate for Rome.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scipio Aemilianus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Scipio Aemilianus</b> finally surrounded the city with a ring of camps and walls and starved it into surrender in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 148 BCE <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b>, acting for Rome, divided royal functions among his three legitimate sons: Micipsa held Cirta and the government, Gulussa the army and Mastanabal justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It ended when <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b>’s forces stormed Carthage after six days of street fighting and razed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 134 BCE <b>Scipio Aemilianus</b> surrounded Numantia with walls and starved it; Retogenes broke out to seek aid, but the town fell in 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/arevaci">Arevaci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-149" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Scipio Aemilianus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-149">What was here in 149 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Lucius Aemilius Paullus Macedonicus</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 229–160 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O Polybius, it is a grand thing, but, I know not how, I feel a terror and dread, lest some one should one day give the same order about my own native city.&#8221;</p><footer>Polybius, Histories, book 38 &#183; tr. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh, 1889 &#183; as he gave the order to burn Carthage, 146 BCE; Polybius, who stood beside him, records it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44126/pg44126-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
